# WP3 — enforced package boundaries, import-cycle detection, and a ratchet

Run 2026-09-23, darwin 25.6.0, Node v22.23.1, pnpm 9.15.0.
Branch `program/developer-trust`, uncommitted working tree, HEAD `b4615721`.

WP3 requirement being evidenced:
  "Add enforced package-dependency boundaries, import-cycle detection, and a ratchet preventing new
  unjustified unsafe casts, suppressions, or unused exports."
  Exit criterion: "package boundaries are enforced".

## 1. What exists now

| Artifact | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `scripts/boundaries-check.mjs` | The gate. Seven rules (R1–R7), pure Node, no dependencies, `--root` / `--baseline` / `--json`. Exits 1 with the offending file named. |
| `scripts/boundaries-baseline.json` | The frozen ratchet. Every number in it was **measured**, written back verbatim from the gate's own `--json` output — not transcribed. |
| `scripts/boundaries-check.test.mjs` | Nine assertion blocks driving the gate against synthetic workspaces in a tmpdir. Each rule is shown FIRING on a violation and QUIET on its compliant twin. |
| `package.json` | `boundaries:check` script; appended to the `verify` chain (after `biome check .`) and to the `installer:test` chain. |

Rules: R1 runtime dependency boundary · R2 test-only devDependency grant · R3 production containment
(no relative escape from a package) · R4 zero package cycles · R5 frozen file-cycle set · R6
suppression ratchet · R7 coverage-debt ratchet.

## 2. The baseline, measured

    $ node scripts/boundaries-check.mjs
    boundaries-check
      baseline                           scripts/boundaries-baseline.json
      packages / source files walked     8 / 535
      declared package edges             20
      package cycles (R4)                0
      file cycles (R5, frozen)           3 / 3
      type-only edges excluded from cycles 652
      JSON data imports (not edges)      22
      unresolved relative specifiers (R7) 3 / 3
      skipped .ts under fixtures/ (R7)   15 / 15
      suppression markers (R6)            cli:2 core:15 mcp:3 memory:1 parsers:11

    PASS — no boundary, cycle, or ratchet violation

**Zero boundary violations and zero package cycles already hold** on this tree. The gate's value here
is that they now *stay* true, and that the three existing file-level cycles are named rather than
discovered later.

The three frozen cycles, none of them new and none fixed by this change:

| Component | Why frozen rather than broken |
| --- | --- |
| `cli/freshness.ts ↔ cli/freshness-child.ts` | parent/child process pair |
| `memory/api.ts → memory/sync/engine.ts → memory/sync/policy.ts` | runs through the public `api` surface |
| `pipeline/parse-pool.ts ↔ parse-concurrent.ts ↔ parse.ts` | shared worker pool |

Each is a refactor with its own blast radius. WP3 asks for **detection and a ratchet**, not a risky
refactor inside a maintenance-hygiene bullet; freezing them makes a *new* cycle fail while leaving
these three to a separately-scoped change. This is stated in the baseline file itself
(`_whyTheThreeCyclesAreFrozenNotFixed`) so a reader does not mistake the freeze for an endorsement.

The 32 suppression markers, measured per package (and per marker kind, so a drop in one kind cannot
mask a rise in another):

    cli  2  (biome-ignore 1, eslint-disable 1)
    core 15 (biome-ignore 9, as any 6)
    mcp  3  (biome-ignore 2, as any 1)
    memory 1 (as any 1)
    parsers 11 (eslint-disable 1, biome-ignore 2, as any 8)
    ────────
    32 total. Zero `@ts-ignore`, zero `@ts-expect-error`, zero `<any>`.

## 3. The gate is not vacuous — shown on synthetic workspaces AND on the real tree

**(a) Synthetic, in `scripts/boundaries-check.test.mjs`** — each rule fires on a violation and stays
quiet on its compliant twin (the pairing is the point: a gate that fails on everything and one that
fails on nothing are equally useless):

    $ node scripts/boundaries-check.test.mjs
    boundaries-check tests ok

Non-vacuity detail worth naming, because two of these are easy to get backwards:
  - **R2's grant is test-only.** The *same* `@knowledge-crib/b` import passes from `one.test.ts` and
    fails from `one.ts` when `b` is a devDependency. Both directions asserted.
  - **R5 tests the baseline, not the existence of a cycle.** One cyclic tree passes when frozen and
    fails when not; and a baseline naming a cycle the tree no longer has ALSO fails — the baseline may
    not outlive the code. Both halves are asserted by message (`NEW import cycle` / `frozen cycle is
    gone or changed`).
  - **R6/R7 ratchets tighten and never loosen.** A count below the baseline passes; one above fails.
  - **The type-only exclusion is a claim about the graph, not a nicety.** A type-only two-file cycle
    is accepted (no runtime cycle) and the 2 excluded edges are counted; the *same two files* with
    value edges DO form a cycle. Without that second assertion the first would also hold for a
    detector that simply never finds cycles.
  - **A `scripts/fixtures/*.mjs` reach from a test file** is permitted by R3 and still counted by R7 —
    a deliberate blind spot is acceptable only as a *named, frozen* number.

**(b) Real tree, real files, nothing modified.** Tightening every ratchet to zero and pointing the gate
at an empty baseline makes it report the real state as violation — which is the strongest available
proof that it is reading the actual repository rather than a fixture. No source file is touched:

    $ node scripts/boundaries-check.mjs --baseline /tmp/tight-baseline.json
    ...
    FAIL — 15 violation(s):
      [R5] NEW import cycle (not in the baseline): packages/cli/src/freshness-child.ts -> packages/cli/src/freshness.ts
      [R5] NEW import cycle (not in the baseline): packages/memory/src/api.ts -> packages/memory/src/sync/engine.ts -> packages/memory/src/sync/policy.ts
      [R5] NEW import cycle (not in the baseline): packages/pipeline/src/parse-concurrent.ts -> packages/pipeline/src/parse-pool.ts -> packages/pipeline/src/parse.ts
      [R6] packages/core now has 9 biome-ignore marker(s) (baseline 0) — …
      [R6] packages/parsers now has 8 as any marker(s) (baseline 0) — …
      [R7] 3 relative specifiers did not resolve (baseline 0) — …
      [R7] 15 .ts files sit under skipped fixtures/ dirs (baseline 0)
    exit=1

15 violations, every one naming a real path in this repository. Combined with (a), the gate is shown
to fire on both synthetic and real inputs.

## 4. What the walk covers, and what it deliberately does not

`packages/<pkg>/src/**` **and** `packages/<pkg>/test/**` — 535 files across 8 packages. `test/` was
added during this run for a measured reason: `packages/cli/test/browser/*.ts` is first-party
executable code that drives the shipped UI in a real browser, and the exit criterion is "all
first-party executable code is covered". Widening the walk added **3 files, 0 new violations, 0 new
suppressions** — verified by re-running before and after, so the coverage gain is real and free.

Outside the walk, counted in the report rather than silently skipped:

| Excluded | Count | Grounds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `packages/<pkg>/fixtures/**` | 15 `.ts` | test DATA, not shipped code (biome ignores them too) |
| `*.config.ts` at package roots | 9 | vitest/playwright config — linted by biome, but not part of the source module graph the boundary rules describe |
| `scripts/**` | — | tooling that deliberately imports built `dist` by path; a package-boundary rule would report its normal behaviour as a violation |
| `packages/ui/web/*.js` | — | browser assets with no module graph |

I checked the walk's blind spot for JS rather than assuming it: the only `.js/.jsx/.mjs/.cjs` under
any `packages/*/src` are four files inside `packages/pipeline/fixtures/plain-js/` — a fixture. **No
shipped `.js` file is outside the walk.**

## 5. Known limits — read these before trusting a green run

- **Textual, not type-aware.** The parser reads import/export specifiers. It follows re-exports
  through barrels (they are `export … from` edges), but it does not resolve tsconfig `paths` aliases
  or see conditional `require`s. **The blind spot has a number: 3 unresolved specifiers**, all of them
  test files deliberately reaching `scripts/fixtures/*.mjs`. R7 fails if that number grows.
- **Type-only edges are excluded from cycle detection** (a type cycle has no runtime effect) and
  counted instead — 652 at freeze. The exclusion is visible, not assumed.
- **JSON imports are data, not edges** — 22 counted, 0 debt. This was a correction made during the
  run: 22 of the first-measured 25 "unresolved" specifiers were `.json` schema imports the resolver
  simply had not been asked to try. A debt figure must mean "the parser cannot see this", not "the
  parser did not look" — otherwise the ratchet would have frozen 22 pieces of false debt.
- **`unused exports` is NOT implemented.** The plan names it in the same bullet as casts and
  suppressions. Proving an export unused needs project-wide symbol analysis (tsc reports unused
  *locals*, not exports); a grep-shaped approximation would fail on prose and be turned off within a
  week. Recorded as an omission in the baseline (`_unusedExportsNotImplemented`) so a green run is not
  read as covering it. **This is the one part of the bullet that is not done, and it is stated as
  such rather than approximated.**
- The gate counts markers, not *justification*. A `biome-ignore` carrying a good reason and one with
  no reason are both 1. The suppression counts are a ratchet on quantity; review is still what
  establishes quality.

## 6. The change is minimal

    $ git diff --numstat -- package.json
    4	3	package.json        # verify + boundary:check + installer:test, 3 changed lines

`package.json` was rewritten by a `JSON.stringify` round-trip during this run, which reformatted the
`keywords` array onto five lines; that unrelated churn was reverted, leaving exactly three changed
lines. New files: `scripts/boundaries-check.mjs`, `scripts/boundaries-check.test.mjs`,
`scripts/boundaries-baseline.json`.

Repo-wide linter after the change (was 699 files before this increment):

    $ npx biome check .
    Checked 702 files in 237ms. No fixes applied.

